---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import LeanBodyMassCalculator from '@/components/tools/calc/LeanBodyMassCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="lean-body-mass-calculator">
  <LeanBodyMassCalculator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the lean body mass calculator works</h2>
    <p>
      Lean body mass is your weight minus your fat: muscle, bone, organs, skin and water. The lean body mass
      calculator estimates it from height and weight with three formulas, and with a fourth when you enter a
      body fat percentage. In each formula <code>W</code> is weight in kg and <code>H</code> is height in cm.
    </p>
    <p>
      Boer (1984): men <code>LBM = 0.407 × W + 0.267 × H − 19.2</code>, women
      <code>LBM = 0.252 × W + 0.473 × H − 48.3</code>. James (1976): men
      <code>LBM = 1.1 × W − 128 × (W ÷ H)²</code>, women <code>LBM = 1.07 × W − 148 × (W ÷ H)²</code>.
      Hume (1966): men <code>LBM = 0.3281 × W + 0.33929 × H − 29.5336</code>, women
      <code>LBM = 0.29569 × W + 0.41813 × H − 43.2933</code>. With a body fat percentage <code>BF</code>:
      <code>LBM = W × (1 − BF ÷ 100)</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Fat mass is <code>W − LBM</code>. The Katch-McArdle equation turns lean mass into resting energy:
      <code>REE = 370 + 21.6 × LBM</code> kcal a day.
    </p>
    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p>
      A man 178 cm tall who weighs 80 kg. Boer gives 0.407 × 80 + 0.267 × 178 − 19.2 = 60.89 kg of lean
      mass, so his fat mass is 19.11 kg, or 23.9% of his weight. James gives 62.15 kg and Hume 57.11 kg.
      Katch-McArdle with the Boer figure gives 370 + 21.6 × 60.89 = 1,685 kcal a day at rest. If a scan
      measures his body fat at 18%, his lean mass is 80 × 0.82 = 65.6 kg.
    </p>
    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <p>
      The three formulas come from adult reference groups and give the average lean mass for a height and
      weight. A muscular person gets too low a value, and a person with much body fat too high a value. The
      James formula gives less lean mass as weight goes up above a BMI of about 43 for men and 36 for women,
      so do not use it there. The formulas are not for children. A good body fat measurement gives a better
      figure than any of them.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
